From 2d8da8ca8df0c68d0279cb5b5671a97c83204659 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viet Hung Nguyen <hvn@calif.io>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 11:10:21 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Optionally bound the JSON nesting depth accepted when
 parsing a schema

serde_json applies a fixed limit of 128 nested containers when parsing schema
JSON, assigned where the `Deserializer` is constructed with no setter to change
it. Apache Avro C++ bounds the same recursion at 600 nested values. A schema
between those two numbers is therefore readable by one implementation and not by
the other, and the crate has no way to move.

Adds `util::set_max_json_depth`, unset by default. Unset means nothing changes at
all: serde_json's own limit stays in force, no depth scan runs, and parsing
behaves byte for byte as it did before. Calling the setter switches both halves
at once -- serde_json's limit off, the scan on -- so exactly one of the two limits
is ever in force and there is no state in which both or neither apply.

The check runs *before* the parse, over the input bytes, and that ordering is the
design rather than an implementation detail. With serde_json's limit disabled the
parse is itself the stack risk, so measuring the depth of the resulting `Value`
would be measuring it after the danger has passed. The scan is a single iterative
pass, aware of string literals and backslash escapes because a `{` inside a `doc`
string is not nesting, so a document nested 60,000 levels deep is refused in
constant stack.

The limit counts nested *values*, not brackets: a value's depth is one plus the
number of containers enclosing it. That is the unit Apache Avro C++ counts in, and
it is one more than a bracket count whenever a scalar sits at the bottom, which in
a schema document it always does -- the innermost thing in a schema is a type name.
So a limit of 600 admits 599 nested containers with a scalar inside them, which is
599 nested arrays or 199 nested one-field records, since a record costs three JSON
levels per Avro level. `set_max_json_depth`'s documentation carries that
conversion table.

The argument is clamped to `MAX_JSON_DEPTH_CEILING`, 600. A limit above what the
stack can hold turns a clean rejection into an abort, and how deep the stack
reaches depends on the build: bisected on aarch64, a document at the limit costs
6.8 MiB of stack unoptimised and 410 KiB optimised. The setter returns what it
installed, so a caller asking for more can see that it was clamped.

Every site that parses schema JSON is covered: four functions and five calls,
since `Schema::parse_str_with_list` parses twice. That includes the container file
header, where `Reader` parses `avro.schema` itself with `serde_json::from_slice`,
so a check inside `Schema::parse_str` would have missed it. At that site a depth
rejection is reported as itself while every other failure keeps reporting
`GetAvroSchemaFromMap`, so nothing but the new rejection changes there.

`util::json_document_end` is public alongside the setter, for a caller that needs
to cut input at the end of its first JSON document. Parsing to find that boundary
is the recursion the limit exists to prevent, so on input that has not been
depth-checked yet it is the wrong order; the scan already tracks everything
finding the boundary requires.

Adds `Details::JsonDepthExceeded`, so the rejection says the depth was exceeded
rather than something about JSON syntax. `Details` has no `#[non_exhaustive]`, so
a new variant is breaking for a consumer matching it exhaustively.

`unbounded_depth` joins serde_json's features, which is what makes
`Deserializer::disable_recursion_limit` available. The feature changes nothing on
its own -- the default stays 128 until something calls the setter -- so it is safe
under Cargo's feature unification.

Tests are split by the value of the setting, because a `OnceLock` cannot be reset
within a process: the unset cases and the scan's own unit tests are in
`src/util.rs`, `tests/max_json_depth.rs` runs at the ceiling, and
`tests/max_json_depth_lowered.rs` runs at a limit the caller chose, which is what
distinguishes a limit that honours its argument from one that always installs 600.
The boundary is pinned as documents rather than as a constant, in two shapes,
because they cost a different number of JSON levels per Avro level and one shape
alone could not show the counter counting the right event.

Verified on apache-avro 0.21 with the six patches before it applied:
`cargo test -p apache-avro --features derive` goes from 567 to 578, the eleven
tests this adds, `cargo fmt --check` is clean, and clippy reports nothing in any
changed file.
